<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<handout>
<title>Line and Surface Integrals</title>
<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>Evaluate the scalar line integral.</p>

<me>
\int_C (x+y)\, ds,
</me>
<p>where <m>C</m> is the curve given by <m>r(t)=\langle t,t^2\rangle,\quad 0\le t\le1</m>.
</p>
<ol>
    <li>Find <m>r'(t)</m>.</li>
    <li>Compute <m>ds</m>.</li>
    <li>Rewrite the integral in terms of <m>t</m>.</li>
    <li>Evaluate.</li>
</ol>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>Find the circulation of the vector field around the unit circle oriented counterclockwise.</p>

<me>
\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
</me>

<md>
\mathbf F=\langle -y,x\rangle
</md>
<ol>
    <li>Parameterize the curve.</li>
    <li>Compute <m>d\mathbf r</m>.</li>
    <li>Rewrite the integral in terms of the parameter.</li>
    <li>Evaluate.</li>
</ol>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>
Consider the vector field
</p>

<me>\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\langle 2x,\,2y\rangle.</me>

<p>
Evaluate the line integral
</p>

<me>\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</me>

<p>
from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(1,1)</m> using two different paths.
Determine whether the integral depends on the path.</p>

<ul>
<li>Straight line path</li>
<li> Path along a curve</li>
</ul>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="2in">
  
        <p>
            Compute the flux of the vector field
        </p>

        <me>\mathbf{F}=\langle x,y,z\rangle</me>

        <p>
            through the portion of the plane
        </p>

        <me>z=2</me>

        <p>
            lying above the square
        </p>

        <me>0\le x\le 1,\qquad 0\le y\le 1.</me>
   

   
        <ol>
            <li>Determine an upward-pointing unit normal vector.</li>
            <li>Compute <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{n}</m> on the surface.</li>
            <li>Set up the flux integral.</li>
            <li>Evaluate the integral.</li>
        </ol>
    

    <hint>
        <p>
            Since the surface is the horizontal plane <m>z=2</m>,
            think what the upward unit normal vector is.
        </p>
    </hint>

   
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>Evaluate a scalar surface integral over a paraboloid <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> over a disk <m>x^2+y^2\le 1</m>.</p>

<me>
\iint_S z\, dS.
</me>

</exercise>
</handout>
